 sections=[
  S("Step 1–2: question, data and physics", "<p>Write a one-line question, for example: <i>predict hourly traffic flow at detector D for the next 24 hours from the past four weeks, scored by mean absolute error</i>. Then plot the data before modelling: daily and weekly patterns, missing hours, sensor failures. List the physics you trust (conservation of vehicles, capacity limits) and the parameters you do not know.</p>",
    ("Frame", "apply", "Question and data first.", [("One-line question", "Target, inputs, metric."), ("Plot everything", "Before modelling."), ("List the physics", "Trusted laws, unknowns.")])),
  S("Step 3: split without leakage", "<p>Leakage is information from the test set sneaking into training. In the explorer, a forecaster that averages the three nearest known hours reports an error of about 90 veh/h with a random split but about 250 veh/h with a time split; its real error next week is about 400 veh/h. The time split is honest; the random split is not. Rules: split time series by time, spatial data by site, and fit any normalisation on training data only.</p>",
    ("Split", "risk", "No peeking.", [("Random split: 90", "Looks great."), ("Time split: 250", "Honest."), ("Next week: 400", "Reality.")])),
  S("Step 4: baselines", "<p>Always build the simplest reasonable model first: yesterday's value at the same hour, a physics model alone, or a linear regression. Report your SciML model's improvement over this baseline. Many published deep-learning results shrink or vanish against a good baseline.</p>",
    ("Baseline", "system", "Set the bar.", [("Persistence", "Same hour yesterday."), ("Physics alone", "No learning."), ("Linear regression", "Simple learning.")])),
  S("The toolchain", "<p><b>Python</b> with <b>NumPy</b> and <b>SciPy</b> for arrays and classical solvers; <b>PyTorch</b> or <b>JAX</b> for neural networks and automatic differentiation; <b>DeepXDE</b>, <b>NVIDIA PhysicsNeMo</b> (formerly Modulus) or <b>NeuroMANCER</b> for physics-informed models; <b>PySINDy</b> for equation discovery; <b>scikit-learn</b> or <b>GPyTorch</b> for Gaussian processes. Use a virtual environment, pin versions, and fix random seeds. Every Go 2 lab has a NumPy version that runs in your browser and, where useful, a PyTorch version to download.</p>",
    ("Tools", "learn", "Python ecosystem.", [("NumPy, SciPy", "Arrays, solvers."), ("PyTorch, JAX", "Autodiff."), ("DeepXDE, PySINDy", "SciML libraries.")])),
 ],
